<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">3</span>
    Looking, Listening, and Writing About the Arts
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">&#9660;</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<h3>How to approach the arts</h3>
<p>Works of art are not puzzles with a hidden answer, and they are not simply matters of taste. They reward attention. Looking at a painting for ten seconds gives you an impression; looking for ten minutes gives you understanding. The same is true of a piece of music, a poem, or a film.</p>
<p>A few things that help: look or listen once without taking notes, then again with a pencil in hand. Notice what your eye or ear goes to first, and ask why. When you finish with a work, try to say in one sentence what it is about and in one more what makes it work. If you cannot, go back.</p>
<p>The lessons will ask you to look closely at many works. Give each your full attention. The more carefully you look, the more you will see.</p>
<h3>How to approach the writing</h3>
<p>Writing about the arts is a skill, and it improves with practice. The most common mistake is vagueness: saying a painting is "beautiful" or "interesting" without saying what specifically makes it so. This course will teach you to be precise about what you see and to link your observations to larger ideas about values, form, and meaning.</p>
<ul>
<li>Read your draft aloud; if a sentence is hard to say, it is probably hard to read</li>
<li>Be specific. Instead of "the painting is powerful," say what makes it powerful: the composition, the color, the subject matter</li>
<li>Make sure each paragraph has a clear purpose and supports your central point</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout callout--note"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">&#8505;&#65039;</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>Keep a journal</strong></p>
<p>Many students find it useful to keep an informal running journal of their reactions to the works they meet, not for a grade, just for themselves. Writing about what you notice trains your eye and your mind at the same time.</p>
</div></div>
