<h3>What academic honesty means in this course</h3>
<p>Academic dishonesty is, broadly speaking, a form of misrepresentation. When you present work that is not genuinely yours, you misrepresent your own understanding, which defeats the purpose of being here. The goal of this course is for you to develop the ability to think critically about the arts and what they reveal about human experience. That cannot happen if someone or something else does the thinking for you.</p>
<h4>Forms of academic dishonesty</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Cheating</strong> includes copying another person's answers or work, or helping others do so.</li>
<li><strong>Fabrication</strong> includes making up sources, quotations, or citations.</li>
<li><strong>Plagiarism</strong> includes presenting someone else's work as your own and copying text from any source without credit.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you are unsure whether something is acceptable, ask before you rely on it, not after.</p>
